      <section class="shell two-column">
        <div><p class="kicker">Infrastructure question</p><h2>How can HMI ideas become testable system behavior without rebuilding the simulator each time?</h2></div>
        <div class="prose"><p>The earlier simulator tightly coupled vehicles, screens, and Unity networking. That made each interface change expensive and limited the lab's ability to run parallel research projects.</p><p>I led a six-member team in establishing a modular replacement: vehicle simulation, ADAS logic, data transport, monitoring, and interface surfaces could evolve independently while remaining synchronized during a study.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="metrics-band"><div class="shell metric-grid"><div><strong>6</strong><span>team members coordinated across system and interface work</span></div><div><strong>30+</strong><span>subsequent HMI and driver-behavior tests supported</span></div><div><strong>5 ADAS</strong><span>ACC, LDW, FCW, BSD, and TSR modules documented</span></div><div><strong>3+ surfaces</strong><span>instrument cluster, center display, HUD, and monitoring endpoints</span></div></div></section>

      <section class="shell design-principles">
        <p class="kicker">System design principles</p><h2>Decouple the components that research teams need to change</h2>
        <div class="principle-grid"><article><span>01</span><h3>Separate function from presentation</h3><p>ADAS logic and interface rendering were managed independently so visual prototypes could change without rewriting vehicle functions.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Publish state, not screen pixels</h3><p>Vehicle position, speed, inputs, and ADAS status were serialized as data and distributed to subscribed endpoints.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Support heterogeneous displays</h3><p>High-performance driving programs and lighter HMI clients could run across PC, Android, and iOS endpoints.</p></article><article><span>04</span><h3>Make experiments observable</h3><p>A monitoring path exposed steering, acceleration, braking, speed, and system state for coordinated study operation.</p></article></div>
      </section>

      <section class="architecture">
        <div class="shell architecture__inner"><div><p class="kicker">Platform architecture</p><h2>A shared event bus synchronized independent research surfaces</h2><p class="prose">MQTT replaced tightly coupled Unity networking. Programs subscribed to the states they needed, allowing the main vehicle, secondary vehicle, displays, and monitor to stay coordinated without becoming one monolithic application.</p></div>
